## Decoding the question
The question states a **premise**: participation has risen, but women still carry **reproductive roles**. **"Discuss the challenges … worldwide, with special reference to Pakistan"** asks for the problems that arise when paid work is **added** to unpaid care — first globally, then in more detail for Pakistan. Use the MCQ terms (glass ceiling, sticky floor, comparable worth) and end with **policy**.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) The premise
Across the world, more women have entered **paid work** in recent decades. But men have not taken on an equal share of **unpaid work** at home. As a result, most working women carry a **double burden**: a paid job plus the housework and care that society still expects of them.

#### ii) The argument
Paid work brings women income and independence, but it also brings serious **challenges** — time poverty, unequal pay, blocked careers, lack of childcare, harassment and insecure jobs. In **Pakistan**, these are sharpened by restricted mobility, social norms and the dominance of informal work. The solution is not to keep women at home but to **share care** and make work **decent**.

### 2. Key terms

#### i) Reproductive roles
**Reproductive roles** are the unpaid work of **bearing and raising children** and **maintaining the household** — cooking, cleaning, fetching water and caring for the sick and elderly.

#### ii) The triple role
**Caroline Moser** (==1993==) described women's **triple role**: **productive** (paid or income-earning) work, **reproductive** work and **community-managing** work.

#### iii) The second shift
The sociologist **Arlie Hochschild** (*The Second Shift*, ==1989==) showed that working women come home to a "**second shift**" of housework and childcare.

### 3. The trend
Globally, women do about **three times** as much unpaid care work as men (**ILO**, ==2018==). In Pakistan, female labour-force participation rose from about ==21.4%== (2020–21) to ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25) — still among the lowest in the world, with large provincial differences: **Punjab** 33.8%, **Balochistan** 14.5%.

### 4. Worldwide challenge 1: the double burden and time poverty
When paid hours are added to unpaid hours, women work **longer total days** than men and have little time for rest, study or leisure — a condition called **time poverty**. Fatigue and stress affect health; some women drop out of paid work when care demands rise, for example after the birth of a child.

### 5. Worldwide challenge 2: unequal pay and comparable worth

#### i) The pay gap
Women earn less than men almost everywhere, partly because they work in lower-paid sectors and fewer hours, and partly because of **discrimination**.

#### ii) Comparable worth
**Comparable worth** means equal pay for work of **equal value**. Where it is denied, "women's jobs" such as nursing, teaching and garment work are paid less than men's jobs of similar skill — "sex segregation and less pay for women's work" (MCQ 2023).

### 6. Worldwide challenge 3: blocked careers

#### i) Occupational segregation
Women are crowded into a narrow range of jobs — care, teaching, clerical and garment work.

#### ii) The glass ceiling
The **glass ceiling** is the set of invisible barriers that stop women rising to the top (MCQ 2024); women hold a small share of senior management and board posts.

#### iii) The sticky floor
The **sticky floor** describes women stuck in **low-wage, low-mobility** jobs with little chance of promotion (MCQ 2026).

### 7. Worldwide challenge 4: motherhood and care

#### i) The motherhood penalty
Mothers often lose pay and promotion after having children, while fathers do not.

#### ii) Childcare
Affordable, good-quality **childcare** is scarce in many countries.

#### iii) Maternity protection
Many women, especially in informal work, have no paid **maternity leave** or job protection.

### 8. Worldwide challenge 5: harassment and insecurity

#### i) Harassment
**Sexual harassment** at work and on the way to work is widespread; the **#MeToo** movement (==2017==) exposed its scale.

#### ii) Insecure work
Many new jobs for women — in export factories, home-based subcontracting and platform work — are **informal**, without contracts, pensions or social security (**Guy Standing**'s "feminisation of labour", ==1989==).

::: fig cycle | The challenges of paid work on top of reproductive roles.
@centre Paid work plus reproductive roles
Double burden and time poverty
Unequal pay; no comparable worth
Segregation, glass ceiling, sticky floor
Motherhood penalty; no childcare
Harassment at work and in transit
Insecure, informal jobs
:::

### 9. The challenges in Pakistan

#### i) Mobility and safety
Unsafe, crowded **public transport** and street **harassment** make getting to work difficult; many families forbid daughters to travel alone.

#### ii) Social norms
The ideal of the **male breadwinner** and family **honour** discourage women's work outside the home; many families accept only teaching or medicine.

#### iii) The joint family
In joint families, working women are often still expected to cook and care for in-laws, so the double burden is heavier.

#### iv) Informality and low pay
Most working women are in **agriculture** or **home-based** piece work, paid little or nothing, with no protection. The ILO has reported one of the **widest gender pay gaps** in the world in Pakistan.

#### v) Harassment at work
The **Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act 2010** (amended ==2022==) requires inquiry committees and created an ombudsperson, but enforcement is weak in the private and informal sectors.

#### vi) Childcare and maternity
Few employers provide **day care**; maternity benefits reach mainly public-sector and formal workers.

#### vii) Educated women leaving work
Many educated women — including large numbers of **doctors** — leave work after marriage because they cannot combine it with family expectations.

#### An example
A woman teacher in a Punjab village who earns her own salary may still cook, clean and care for children and in-laws before and after school — the **double burden** in its sharpest form. Without childcare, transport or help at home, many such women give up work after marriage or the birth of a first child, which helps explain why female participation is only ==24.4%==.

### 10. The benefits of paid work
Despite these challenges, paid work brings women **income, independence, confidence and a stronger voice** at home. **Naila Kabeer**'s research on Bangladeshi garment workers (*The Power to Choose*, ==2000==) found that paid work delayed marriage and increased women's bargaining power. The goal is therefore to remove the challenges, not to keep women out of work.

### 11. Laws and policies in Pakistan
Pakistan has taken some steps: the **Harassment Act 2010**; a **10% quota** for women in federal services; the **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act 2018** and **Sindh Maternity Benefits Act 2018**; the **Punjab Domestic Workers Act 2019**; and the federal **Maternity and Paternity Leave Act 2020**, which extended maternity leave and introduced paternity leave. Coverage of informal workers remains the main gap.

### 12. Way forward and conclusion

#### i) Way forward
Provide **safe transport** and enforce harassment laws; build **workplace day care** and public crèches; introduce **paternity leave** and campaigns for men to share housework; enforce **equal pay for work of equal value**; extend protection to **home-based and domestic** workers; count **unpaid care** through time-use surveys (**SDG target 5.4**).

#### ii) Conclusion
Women's entry into paid work is progress, but it has come **without a matching change in reproductive roles**. Worldwide and in Pakistan, the result is a double burden, unequal pay, blocked careers and insecurity — made worse in Pakistan by restricted mobility, social norms and informality. The answer is to **recognise, reduce and redistribute** care and to make work decent, so that paid employment empowers women rather than exhausting them.

## Why this answer scores
- Builds from the question's **premise** (more paid work, same reproductive roles).
- Covers **worldwide** challenges and then **Pakistan** in more depth, as instructed.
- Uses the examiner's own MCQ concepts — **glass ceiling, sticky floor, comparable worth**.
- A **cycle figure**, Pakistani **laws** and a **policy** section complete a balanced answer.
